@bio:

Emili Ros-Fàbregas was a former researcher in Musicology
<i>ad honorem</i> at the Spanish National Research Council (CSIC),
Institución Milá y Fontanals of Research in Humanities
(IMF-CSIC) in Barcelona (2009-retired in 2026). He obtained
his Ph.D. in Musicology (1992) at the Graduate Center, The
City University of New York (CUNY; Allan W. Atlas,
advisor), and taught at Brooklyn College in New York, at
Boston University (where he conducted the Collegium
Musicum), and —after twenty years abroad— at the
Universidad de Granada, and Universitat de Girona in Spain;
he was also invited to undertake research at University of
Cambridge in the UK and the University of Chicago. His
research focuses on Spanish music of the Renaissance, the
historiography of Spanish music in an international
context, and the recent developments of Digital Musicology.
He created the open access platforms <i>Fondo de Música
Tradicional IMF-CSIC</i> (in 2012) and <i>Books of Hispanic
Polyphony</i> (in 2013). Elected member of Academia Europaea
(2013), he is director of the journal <i>Anuario Musical</i> since
2019.

As member of the Core Group and co-leader of Working Group
2 devoted to “Sources”, he has participated in the COST
Action “A new ecosystem of early music studies,
EarlyMuse, 2023-2026” (directed by Philippe Vendrix). He
is currently president of the Catalan Musicological
Society, and for the next four years he has been elected to
the Board of the choral institution Orfeó Català —owner
of the Barcelona modernist concert hall Palau de la Música
Catalana— in an advisory position as Archivist-Librarian
of the Center for Documentation and Research of the Orfeó
Català (CEDOC), one of the major musicological archives in
Catalonia. His publications are available in
Academia.edu/EmilioRosFabregas.


@@END: BIO